### TITLE ###
UfcsSortPreservesElementType
### DESCRIPTION ###
The single-list UFCS form `items.sort()` threads the receiver in as the
implicit first argument, so refinement preserves the element type (str[])
just like the call form `sort(items)`. Iterating then yields str.
### INPUT ###
items = ["b", "a"]
items = items.sort()
for v in items:
    split(v, ",")
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  items @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  items @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  items @ 2:9 -> str[]
  sort @ 2:15 -> dynamic
  items @ 3:10 -> str[]
  split @ 4:5 -> dynamic
  v @ 4:11 -> str

# Symbol types
  items (local): str[]
  v (loop): str

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
LockstepSortIsATupleOfItsArguments
### DESCRIPTION ###
Lockstep sort (`sort(xs, ys)`) reorders every list by the primary's ordering
and returns them as a list-of-lists - so the result is precisely a tuple of
the argument types, and a destructuring capture keeps each list's element
type. The signature can only say `list|str`, which used to leave `a` as
`dynamic|str` and `b` as `dynamic|null`: the str arm then failed any
list-typed parameter, and scripts kept an unsorted copy under a second name
purely to satisfy the checker.
### INPUT ###
xs = [3, 1, 2]
ys = ["c", "a", "b"]
a, b = sort(xs, ys)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  xs @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  ys @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  a @ 3:1 -> <no-type>
  b @ 3:4 -> <no-type>
  sort @ 3:8 -> dynamic
  xs @ 3:13 -> int[]
  ys @ 3:17 -> str[]

# Symbol types
  a (local): int[]
  b (local): str[]
  xs (local): int[]
  ys (local): str[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
LockstepSortWithNonListArgStaysWide
### DESCRIPTION ###
Negative case for the tuple refinement. A str isn't a reorderable column, so
`sort(xs, s)` isn't the lockstep shape - refinement must not manufacture a
tuple from arguments it can't vouch for. The result stays the signature's
wide `list|str`, which distributes as dynamic|str / dynamic|null.
### INPUT ###
xs = [3, 1, 2]
s = "cab"
a, b = sort(xs, s)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  xs @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  s @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  a @ 3:1 -> <no-type>
  b @ 3:4 -> <no-type>
  sort @ 3:8 -> dynamic
  xs @ 3:13 -> int[]
  s @ 3:17 -> str

# Symbol types
  a (local): dynamic|str
  b (local): dynamic|null
  s (local): str
  xs (local): int[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
UfcsClampFloatReceiverStaysFloat
### DESCRIPTION ###
`v.clamp(0, 10)` on a float receiver must include the receiver when deciding
all-int collapse: the float receiver means the result is int|float, not the
wrongly-collapsed int it would be if only the written args (0, 10) were seen.
### INPUT ###
v = 1.5
r = v.clamp(0, 10)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  v @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  r @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  v @ 2:5 -> float
  clamp @ 2:7 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  r (local): int|float
  v (local): float

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
UfcsClampIntReceiverCollapsesToInt
### DESCRIPTION ###
With an all-int receiver and args, `n.clamp(0, 10)` collapses to int - the
receiver participates in the all-int check, so the precise int return is
recovered for the UFCS form too.
### INPUT ###
n = 5
r = n.clamp(0, 10)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  n @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  r @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  n @ 2:5 -> int
  clamp @ 2:7 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  n (local): int
  r (local): int

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
ReadFileContentIsStrByDefault
### DESCRIPTION ###
`read_file`'s `content` is declared `str|int[]` because mode decides it, and
mode is named-only with a "text" default. Resolving the default means the
overwhelmingly common `read_file(p).content` types as a plain str and flows
into str-typed parameters, instead of dragging an int[] arm nobody can hit.
### INPUT ###
content = read_file("f.txt").content
split(content, "\n")
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  content @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  read_file @ 1:11 -> dynamic
  split @ 2:1 -> dynamic
  content @ 2:7 -> str

# Symbol types
  content (local): str

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
ReadFileBytesModeContentIsIntList
### DESCRIPTION ###
Negative case: an explicit `mode="bytes"` must refine the other way, not
default to str. Reading the written kwarg (not just the signature default) is
what makes the refinement honest rather than a blanket assumption.
### INPUT ###
content = read_file("f.bin", mode="bytes").content
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  content @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  read_file @ 1:11 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  content (local): int[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
ReadFileNonLiteralModeStaysWide
### DESCRIPTION ###
Negative case: the checker does no constant folding, so a mode it can't read
statically must keep the wide `str|int[]`. Guessing "text" here would be the
failure the allow-list exists to prevent - a refinement that makes the checker
miss a real error.

The RAD30001 hint below is unrelated and pre-existing: a plain str flowing
into a str-enum parameter can't be proven a member without singleton types,
which the narrower deliberately doesn't mint (see narrow.go). It's pinned
here only because it shares the snippet.
### INPUT ###
m = "bytes"
content = read_file("f.bin", mode=m).content
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  m @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  content @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  read_file @ 2:11 -> dynamic
  m @ 2:35 -> str

# Symbol types
  content (local): str|int[]
  m (local): str

# Diagnostics
  [hint] RAD30001 @ 2:35 - Argument type 'str' is not assignable to expected type '["text", "bytes"]'

### TITLE ###
MapTakesElementTypeFromItsLambda
### DESCRIPTION ###
`map`'s signature can only say `map|list`. The result kind follows the
collection, and the element type follows the lambda's inferred return - so
mapping a list of pairs to a string yields str[], not a union that fails
every list-typed parameter.
### INPUT ###
pairs = [["a", "b"], ["c", "d"]]
labels = pairs.map(fn(x) "{x[0]}/{x[1]}")
for label in labels:
    split(label, "/")
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  pairs @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  labels @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  pairs @ 2:10 -> str[][]
  map @ 2:16 -> dynamic
  x @ 2:28 -> <no-type>
  x @ 2:35 -> <no-type>
  labels @ 3:14 -> str[]
  split @ 4:5 -> dynamic
  label @ 4:11 -> str

# Symbol types
  label (loop): str
  labels (local): str[]
  pairs (local): str[][]
  x (param): <no-type>

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
MapOverAMapPreservesKeys
### DESCRIPTION ###
Mapping a map rewrites values and keeps keys, so the result is a map from the
same key type to the lambda's return type.
### INPUT ###
scores = {"a": 1, "b": 2}
doubled = scores.map(fn(k, v) v * 2)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  scores @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  doubled @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  scores @ 2:11 -> { str: int }
  map @ 2:18 -> dynamic
  v @ 2:31 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  doubled (local): { str: dynamic }
  scores (local): { str: int }
  v (param): <no-type>

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
MapOverUnknownCollectionStaysWide
### DESCRIPTION ###
Negative case: with no static handle on the collection's kind there's nothing
to refine, so the signature's `map|list` stands.
### INPUT ###
data = parse_json("[]")
mapped = data.map(fn(x) x)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  data @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  parse_json @ 1:8 -> dynamic
  mapped @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  data @ 2:10 -> any
  map @ 2:15 -> dynamic
  x @ 2:25 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  data (local): any
  mapped (local): map|list
  x (param): <no-type>

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
FilterPreservesTheCollectionType
### DESCRIPTION ###
`filter` keeps a subset of what it was given - same kind, same elements, only
the length changes, and types don't track length. So the argument's own type
is exact.
### INPUT ###
nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]
evens = nums.filter(fn(x) x % 2 == 0)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  nums @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  evens @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  nums @ 2:9 -> int[]
  filter @ 2:14 -> dynamic
  x @ 2:27 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  evens (local): int[]
  nums (local): int[]
  x (param): <no-type>

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
ReversePreservesTheCollectionType
### DESCRIPTION ###
`reverse` is the same shape as single-arg `sort`, which was already refined -
leaving the two inconsistent was an accident, not a decision.
### INPUT ###
names = ["a", "b"]
flipped = names.reverse()
for n in flipped:
    split(n, ",")
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  names @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  flipped @ 2:1 -> <no-type>
  names @ 2:11 -> str[]
  reverse @ 2:17 -> dynamic
  flipped @ 3:10 -> str[]
  split @ 4:5 -> dynamic
  n @ 4:11 -> str

# Symbol types
  flipped (local): str[]
  n (loop): str
  names (local): str[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
RangeOfIntsIsIntList
### DESCRIPTION ###
The list counterpart of the clamp/min/max/abs all-int collapse: all-int
arguments can't produce floats, so `float[]|int[]` narrows to int[]. An
omitted `_step` defaults to the int literal 1, so it needs no special care.
### INPUT ###
xs = range(0, 10)
for x in xs:
    abs(x)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  xs @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  range @ 1:6 -> dynamic
  xs @ 2:10 -> int[]
  abs @ 3:5 -> dynamic
  x @ 3:9 -> int

# Symbol types
  x (loop): int
  xs (local): int[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
RangeWithFloatStepStaysWide
### DESCRIPTION ###
Negative case: one float argument puts floats back in play, so the wide
return stands.
### INPUT ###
xs = range(0, 1, 0.25)
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  xs @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  range @ 1:6 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  xs (local): float[]|int[]

# Diagnostics
  (none)

### TITLE ###
RefinedReadFileStillReportsAsFallible
### DESCRIPTION ###
Refinement runs before the fallible-call strip, so a refiner that rebuilt
read_file's return without its error arm would silently switch off RAD30011
for every read_file in the language - a refinement quietly disabling a
diagnostic is exactly the regression that's hard to notice. Under --strict
the unhandled-fallible warning must still fire on the refined call.
### INPUT ###
content = read_file("f.txt").content
### ARGS ###
--strict
### STDOUT ###
# Identifier types
  content @ 1:1 -> <no-type>
  read_file @ 1:11 -> dynamic

# Symbol types
  content (local): str

# Diagnostics
  [warn] RAD30011 @ 1:11 - This call can fail; the error isn't handled and would halt the script
    help: Handle it with `catch` or `??`, e.g. `x = <call> catch <fallback>`
